Volunteer Dinner
===================================

The Hugo website for the annual free community dinner in Montreal.

Development
-----------

Install Hugo (0.158.0 or later; tested with 0.162.1). Standard Hugo is sufficient;
there are no theme submodules, JavaScript packages, external fonts, or Sass
dependencies.

Production builds also require golang 1.25 or later for the PDF poster generator.
Its dependencies are pinned in tools/poster/go.mod and go.sum. Fonts are embedded
from golang.org/x/image, so no browser, Python, or system font installation is
needed. The first build downloads the golang modules.

	make server

English is at http://localhost:1313/ and French at http://localhost:1313/fr/.
Build the production site with:

	make build

Generated files are in public/. Do not edit those files directly.
The logo and favicon live in static/images/ and are copied unchanged.
The original hall photo lives in assets/images/; Hugo creates a 1200-pixel-wide
version for the homepage and social previews. CSS lives in assets/css/ and
is minified and fingerprinted by Hugo.
The 2024 dinner photo comes from the original project page.

Poster
------

The home and dinner pages link to /poster/ (French: /fr/poster/), where visitors
can download /poster.pdf. This is a single bilingual Letter-size page, like the
original flyer. Both languages download the same PDF.

`make build` runs Hugo first, exporting the poster data to /poster/index.json,
then runs the isolated golang module in tools/poster/ to draw public/poster.pdf
and public/poster.svg. The SVG thumbnail on the poster page uses the same drawing
commands and embedded fonts as the PDF, so it stays in sync without a separate
conversion tool.
GitHub Actions runs this same command before uploading the Pages artifact, so
each push to master rebuilds and publishes the poster along with the website.
Running Hugo alone, including `make server`, does not regenerate the PDF; run
`make build` after edits to preview the download.

Update params.activeYear in config.toml and the date, hours, venue, and address
in data/dinner.toml for each edition. Contact details come from data/contact.toml.
Poster wording lives alongside the other translations in i18n/en.toml and
i18n/fr.toml, under the poster_* keys. The layout and palette live
in tools/poster/main.go; they are drawn directly rather than interpreted from
website CSS. The generator measures text and fails the build if it cannot fit.
Do not edit or commit the generated PDF or JSON.

Content and translations
------------------------

The active dinner year lives in config.toml under params.activeYear. Update it
for each edition; both languages use it in the next-dinner summaries.
Other event details live in data/dinner.toml. Keep the date, venue, and hours
current in both languages, along with the When and where section on the dinner
pages. The street address can be added when needed.

The volunteer sign-up form URL lives in config.toml under
params.volunteerSignupURL. Both languages use this URL for their sign-up buttons.
Publish the form before announcing sign-ups.

The donation sign-up form URL lives in config.toml under params.donationSignupURL.
It covers turkeys, desserts, and individual monetary donations. Both languages
use this URL for the donation button when it is set. Leave it empty to keep the
email button until the form is ready.

The public contact address lives in data/contact.toml. General inquiries and
the fallback donation button open an email to this address. Arrange turkey
preparation and delivery directly with the organizing team.

Before announcing a dinner, confirm its details, check the public contact,
review both translations, and add any current flyer or sponsor acknowledgements.
Do not promise registration arrangements, accessibility, delivery, dietary
accommodations, or donation receipts without confirmation from the team.

Donor acknowledgements
---------------------

Donor records live in data/donors/<year>.json, one file per year. Each file
contains organizations and individuals arrays. The pages list years from
newest to oldest, with companies and organizations first and individual donors
underneath. Empty groups are hidden.

Every entry is published, so add only donors who have opted in. There is no
consent flag and no placeholder data.

Organization entries have a translated name (en and fr), optional translated
type and description fields, an optional url, and an optional logo path
relative to static/. Individual entries contain only an untranslated name:

	{"name": "James (@purpleidea)"}

The English and French donor pages are linked from the donation pages.
They have no header or footer menu entries.

Sources
-------

General information: https://purpleidea.com/projects/dinner/
History and meal counts: https://purpleidea.com/misc/dinner/

Deployment
----------

The GitHub Pages workflow builds pull requests without deploying. A push to
master or a manual workflow run on master builds and deploys the site.
Configure the repository's Pages source as GitHub Actions and its custom domain
as volunteerdinner.net; static/CNAME supplies that domain in the generated site.
Publishing requires the repository and domain configuration to be in place.

Use a branch for changes. Local reference material under tmp/ is ignored.
